<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-inverse-laplace" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Inverse Laplace Transform</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       DEFINITION: INVERSE LAPLACE TRANSFORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Knowing <m>F(s)</m> uniquely determines <m>f(t)</m>. This lets us define the inverse.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-inverse-laplace">
    <title>The Inverse Laplace Transform</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given <m>F(s)</m>, the <term>inverse Laplace transform</term> is the function <m>f(t)</m>
        such that <m>\mathcal{L}\{f(t)\} = F(s)</m>. We write:
        <me>f(t) = \mathcal{L}^{-1}\{F(s)\}.</me>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    In practice, we find inverse Laplace transforms by reading the table backwards: given <m>F(s)</m>,
    we look for a row whose right column matches, then read off the <m>f(t)</m> in the left column.
    The inverse Laplace transform is also linear, so we can split over sums and pull constants out
    front just as before.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE: L^{-1}{1/(s+5)}
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

 <example xml:id="ex-inverse-laplace-basic">
    <title>A Basic Inverse Laplace Transform</title>
    <p>
      Compute <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\dfrac{1}{s+5}\right\}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      We look for a row in the table whose <m>F(s)</m> column matches <m>\dfrac{1}{s+5}</m>.
      This fits Formula B with <m>a = -5</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow>\mathcal{L}\{e^{at}\} = \frac{1}{s-a} \quad \Longrightarrow \quad
        \mathcal{L}\{e^{-5t}\} = \frac{1}{\underbrace{s-(-5)}_{s+5}} = \frac{1}{s+5}.</mrow>
      </md>
      Reading backwards:
      <me>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{1}{s+5}\right\} = e^{-5t}.</me>
    </p>
  </example>
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       PROSE: THE REWRITING TRICK
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

<p>
    Sometimes the expression doesn't immediately match a row in the table. Consider
    <m>\dfrac{2}{s^2+9}</m>. The denominator <m>s^2 + 9 = s^2 + 3^2</m> tells us
    this is related to Formula D,
    <me>\mathcal{L}\{\sin(at)\} = \frac{a}{s^2+a^2},</me>
    with <m>a = 3</m>. But Formula D requires a 3 in the numerator, and we only have a 2.
    <me>\frac{\overbrace{2}^{\text{needs to be } 3}}{s^2+\underbrace{9}_{3^2 = 9 \Rightarrow a=3}}</me>
    The key steps to handle this are:
    <ol>
      <li><p>Identify <m>a</m> from the denominator: <m>s^2 + 9 = s^2 + 3^2</m>, so <m>a = 3</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>We need <m>a = 3</m> in the numerator, so multiply and divide by 3.</p></li>
      <li><p>Pull the leftover constant out front using linearity.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CHECKPOINT: CORRECT REWRITE OF 2/(s^2+9)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-inverse-laplace-checkpoint">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Following the steps above, which of the following correctly rewrites
        <m>\dfrac{2}{s^2+9}</m> to match Formula D?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><m>\dfrac{2}{3} \cdot \dfrac{3}{s^2+3^2}</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! Since <m>a = 3</m>, we need a 3 in the numerator.
            Multiplying and dividing by 3 gives <m>\frac{2}{3} \cdot \frac{3}{s^2+3^2}</m>,
            which matches Formula D with the constant <m>\frac{2}{3}</m> out front.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>2 \cdot \dfrac{1}{s^2+3^2}</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Formula D requires <m>a = 3</m> in the numerator, not 1.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>\dfrac{2}{9} \cdot \dfrac{9}{s^2+3^2}</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            We need a 3 in the numerator to match Formula D with <m>a = 3</m>, not a 9.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>\dfrac{2}{3} \cdot \dfrac{3}{s^2+9^2}</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The constant out front is right, but the denominator should be <m>s^2 + 3^2</m>,
            not <m>s^2 + 9^2</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACTIVITY: L^{-1}{2/(s^2+9)}
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <activity xml:id="act-inverse-laplace-rewrite">
    <title>Compute <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\dfrac{2}{s^2+9}\right\}</m></title>

    <task xml:id="task-inverse-laplace-apply">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the rewrite from the checkpoint, compute <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\dfrac{2}{s^2+9}\right\}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Use the rewrite <m>\dfrac{2}{s^2+9} = \dfrac{2}{3} \cdot \dfrac{3}{s^2+3^2}</m>
          and apply the inverse transform.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
    
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{2}{s^2+9}\right\}
              \amp= \frac{2}{3}\,\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{3}{s^2+3^2}\right\}
               = \frac{2}{3}\sin(3t).</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <solution>
          <title>More detail on the second equality</title>
          <p>
            The second factor <m>\dfrac{3}{s^2+3^2}</m> matches
            <xref ref="formula-D" text="custom">Formula D</xref>,
            <m>\mathcal{L}\{\sin(at)\} = \dfrac{a}{s^2+a^2}</m>, with <m>a = 3</m>.
            Reading backwards: <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\dfrac{3}{s^2+3^2}\right\} = \sin(3t)</m>.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </activity>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>The inverse Laplace transform</alert> reads the table backwards: given <m>F(s)</m>,
          find the matching row and read off <m>f(t)</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>It is also linear:</alert>
          <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\{aF + bG\} = a\mathcal{L}^{-1}\{F\} + b\mathcal{L}^{-1}\{G\}</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Rewriting is often needed:</alert> identify <m>a</m> from the denominator,
          then multiply and divide to get <m>a</m> in the numerator, and carry the leftover constant out front.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

